Affected versions: present on the `main` branch at commit `791a352dee3b3d8c6f64edcaf229ce474a68f6c5`. Not present in the latest tagged release, `v0.2.4`, which predates both the `openclawSourceDir` field and the current `ipcHandlers/skills/handlers.ts` module entirely; this appears to be a `main`-branch-only, pre-release issue.

## Summary

An installed skill's own `_meta.json` file can contain an arbitrary `openclawSourceDir` field. When the user later uninstalls that skill, the `skills:delete` IPC handler reads this field directly from the skill's `_meta.json` on disk and recursively deletes whatever path it names, with no containment check, no canonicalization, and no verification that the value came from the application itself rather than from the installed skill package.

## Details

`src/main/ipcHandlers/skills/handlers.ts`'s `skills:delete` handler:

```ts
let openclawSourceDir: string | null = null;
try {
  const skillRoot = getSkillManager().getSkillsRoot();
  const metaPath = path.join(skillRoot, id, '_meta.json');
  if (fs.existsSync(metaPath)) {
    const meta = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(metaPath, 'utf8'));
    if (meta.openclawSourceDir) {
      openclawSourceDir = meta.openclawSourceDir;
    }
  }
} catch { /* best-effort */ }

const skills = await getSkillManager().deleteSkill(id);

if (openclawSourceDir && fs.existsSync(openclawSourceDir)) {
  try {
    await fs.promises.rm(openclawSourceDir, { recursive: true, force: true });
    ...
```

`openclawSourceDir` is meant to track the location of a skill synced in from the OpenClaw workspace, so that deleting the LobsterAI-managed copy also cleans up the original. The only code that legitimately sets this field is `syncSkillsFromOpenClaw` in `src/main/skills/skillManager.ts`, which writes it from a path the OpenClaw gateway's own report supplied.

However, a skill installed through the ordinary download/import path (`downloadSkill`, `upgradeSkill`) reaches the managed skills directory via `cpRecursiveSync(skillDir, targetDir)`, a plain recursive file copy of the entire downloaded package. If that package already contains its own `_meta.json` with an `openclawSourceDir` field (nothing requires it not to), that file is copied over unchanged, and the delete handler has no way to distinguish it from a legitimately recorded one.

The pre-install security scan (`skillSecurityScanner.ts`) does not provide a safety net here: it only inspects `.js`/`.ts` script files, `SKILL.md`, and `package.json`. It never reads `_meta.json`, so a package with this field and no other suspicious content is never flagged, even under the scanner's "risky, needs user confirmation" path.

## POC

(available upon request)

## Impact

A skill package with an otherwise entirely benign `SKILL.md` and no suspicious scripts, so nothing for the pre-install security scanner to flag, can ship a `_meta.json` naming any absolute path the installing user can write to: their home directory, a source tree, an application data directory, anything. When that user later uninstalls the skill through the normal UI flow, the named path is recursively and permanently deleted. The only user actions required are installing a skill and later uninstalling it, both routine, expected product usage, not an elevated or unusual capability.

Suggested fix: never read deletion-authority metadata from a location the installed package itself controls. Store the OpenClaw-source-directory association in the application's own store (for example, alongside the existing per-skill `skills_state` key), written only by the code path that legitimately establishes it, and read from that store on delete instead of from the skill's own `_meta.json`. A fix implementing exactly this is included in the linked pull request, along with a regression test.

### Affected component

runtime/daemon

### Severity

S3 - minor issue

(No code on `master` reads these columns today, so there is no visible symptom yet; the stored values are already wrong. See "Impact" for a draft PR that would start showing them.)

### Current behavior

`AcpSessionStore::replace_messages_inner` (`crates/zeroclaw-infra/src/acp_session_store.rs:1943`) replaces the whole transcript of an ACP session. It takes one `let now = Utc::now().to_rfc3339();` (`:1949`), runs `DELETE FROM acp_messages WHERE session_id = ?1` (`:1956`, which cascades to the session's `acp_tool_calls` rows, comment at `:1960`) and reinserts everything through `insert_messages(&tx, session_id, messages, &now)` (`:1964`). That one `now` becomes `created_at` of every chat row (`:1625-1628`), every assistant tool-call row (`:1653-1661`), and every tool-call `'in'` and `'out'` row (`:1669-1678`, `:1747-1758`). After a replace, every row of the session carries the time of the replace, including rows that were persisted earlier with their own time. Row ids are renumbered too (a test pins that, `cursor_rejects_transcript_replacement_and_fresh_request_recovers`, `:5196`).

When it runs: `AcpServer::replace_transcript` (`crates/zeroclaw-channels/src/orchestrator/acp_server.rs:2837`, calling `replace_messages_and_breadcrumb` at `:2847`) is called only when the live history was trimmed: at turn end (`:2101` on success, `:2128` on cancel, `:2192`), gated by `history_trim_generation() != history_trim_generation_before_turn` (`:2097`, `:2121`), and on `session/load` / `session/resume` when the restore trims (`:1342`, `:1669`). Other turns append the delta instead (`append_transcript`, `:2778`). The `AcpServer` is used by standalone `zeroclaw acp` (`src/main.rs:7432`) and the gateway WebSocket ACP (`crates/zeroclaw-gateway/src/acp.rs:87`, `:100`); `docs/book/src/channels/acp.md:262` says both "persist terminal turn state". The ZeroCode/daemon RPC path does not replace the transcript (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/rpc/dispatch.rs:7162-7164`: "falling back to a wholesale transcript replacement here would erase the append-only journal frontier"); the only other caller of `replace_messages_and_breadcrumb` there, `persist_acp_turn` (`dispatch.rs:13273`), is `#[cfg(test)]`.

So the per-message time is kept for a session until its first trim and flattened at every trim after that. The `append_turn` path keeps `created_at` and row ids of earlier rows (control test below), which is what makes the replace path inconsistent with the other writer of the same store.

No code on `master` reads the columns: the `SELECT`s over `acp_messages` request `id, role, content, reasoning_content` (`acp_session_store.rs:936-937`, `:1373-1374`), `ConversationMessage` has no time field (`crates/zeroclaw-api/src/model_provider.rs:279`), and the `created_at` reads in this file are of `acp_sessions`. An operator can still query the column by hand (the SQL pasted in #10788 does). The draft PR #11622 would add `created_at` to those `SELECT`s and show it in ZeroCode; it fixes the prompt row's time for checkpointed turns only and leaves this replace path unchanged.

The same mechanism, in the `sessions` store, is #11420.

### Expected behavior

Proposal, following the principle stated for the `sessions` store in #11420 (`created_at` = when the message was first persisted): rows that survive a replace keep their known `created_at`, only new rows get a new one, and already-overwritten values are not invented back. Open points for ACP that #11420 does not decide: how rows are matched (typed `ConversationMessage` rows instead of flat chat messages, plus `acp_tool_calls` child rows that hang off the last assistant message), whether tool-call rows follow the same rule, and the row-id renumbering that the cursor test relies on. The transactional replace and the invalidation of the retained-context / projected-count / watermark in the same transaction (`:1975-1989`) must stay as they are.

### Steps to reproduce

Prerequisites: a Linux or WSL checkout of `ea4a54bb5bd77dd56fb76e919754bc5d63a099c3` (`origin/master` on 2026-10-12) and Rust >= the `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml`. No configuration is needed; the tests use a temporary directory.

1. Take a disposable copy of the tree (the next step edits a source file):
   ```sh
   mkdir ~/zc-acp-repro && git -C <repo> archive ea4a54bb5bd77dd56fb76e919754bc5d63a099c3 | tar -x -C ~/zc-acp-repro
   ```
2. Append the four tests below at the end of the `tests` module of `crates/zeroclaw-infra/src/acp_session_store.rs` (the last line of that file, `}`, closes `mod tests`; remove it, paste the tests, add it back). They use `open_store()`, `create_session`, `append_turn`, `replace_messages`, `replace_messages_and_breadcrumb`, and `store.conn.lock()` for direct SQL because no reader exposes these columns. Predictions were written before running: the first three fail on a `created_at` assertion and the last one (control) passes.
3. Run:
   ```sh
   cd ~/zc-acp-repro
   cargo test -p zeroclaw-infra --locked --lib -- --nocapture --test-threads=1 replace_messages_keeps_created_at_of_unchanged_rows rv_
   ```
4. Expected after a fix: the first three tests pass (the control already does). Obtained today: `1 passed; 3 failed`.

